uise--ImageViewerWidget {
qproperty-controlsFadeInDurationMs: 150;
qproperty-controlsFadeOutDurationMs: 300;
qproperty-controlsMaxOpacity: 1.0;
}

/* ControlsMode::Overlay: the embedded controlsFrame (and, when a caller supplies its own bottom
 * widget via setBottomWidget(), that widget too, via its own styling) floats over the image
 * instead of sitting in normal layout flow below it -- see
 * ImageViewerWidget::applyControlsMode(), which sets this "overlay" dynamic property. It needs
 * a backing of its own to stay legible over arbitrary image content, same rationale as
 * ChatMessageImageItem's #menuButton translucent backing in chatmessagefiles.qss. Deliberately
 * theme-independent (always a dark backing) rather than following light/dark.qss, since it
 * always sits over a photo rather than the app's own background.
 *
 * Known limitation: the embedded toolbar's icons still come from the ImageEditor context
 * (imageeditor.json), whose colors are tuned for sitting on the app's normal background, not
 * this dark overlay backing -- in the light theme they read with reduced contrast here. A
 * caller that cares (see ChatImageViewerControls, which ships its own always-light icon set
 * instead of the embedded toolbar) should supply a custom bottom widget via setBottomWidget()
 * rather than relying on the embedded one for prolonged overlay use.
 */
uise--ImageViewerWidget[overlay="true"] #controlsFrame {
background-color: rgba(0,0,0,140);
border-radius: 8px;
}
